{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KSzTSomifHWUeh85XFcxvj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCITATION RULES — [@Key] & BibLaTeX OUTPUTS\n2\t\n3\tGoal\n4\t- Define how to represent citations in documents that use [@Key] syntax with a matching BibLaTeX database.\n5\t- Convert token citations from upstream workflows into standard [@Key] form and ensure a complete biblatex.bib when such output is requested.\n6\t\n7\t\n8\tCitation Representation (MANDATORY)\n9\t- When these rules apply, the final document MUST use `[@Key]` citation syntax only.\n10\t- Source tokens (from upstream workflows):\n11\t  - `[KB:filename.md]`\n12\t  - `[WEB:https://...]`\n13\t  - `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`\n14\t  - `[ARXIV:paper-id]`\n15\t  - `[ZOTERO:item_key]` (optional)\n16\t- During compilation:\n17\t  - CONVERT all supported tokens to `[@Key]` form.\n18\t  - Do NOT leave any token citations (`[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`) in the final document.\n19\t  - It is acceptable to keep raw URLs in prose **in addition to** [@Key] when the URL itself is content (e.g., pointing to an online tool), but the citation must still use [@Key].\n20\t\n21\tCitekey Rules\n22\t- Citekeys MUST follow the pattern: `^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]{2,64}$` (letters and digits only; start with a letter).\n23\t- Key format by number of authors:\n24\t  - 1 author: `AuthorYear` (e.g., `Kramer2015`)\n25\t  - 2 authors: `Author1Author2Year` (e.g., `WellsCoppersmith1994`, `AtkinsonBoore2006`)\n26\t  - 3+ authors: `FirstAuthorEtAlYear` (e.g., `BooreEtAl2014`, `AbrahamsonEtAl2016`)\n27\t  - Organization/committee: `OrganizationYear` (e.g., `SSHAC1997`, `ANCOLD2019`)\n28\t- For collisions (same key pattern): append a, b, c (e.g., `Boore2014a`, `Boore2014b`).\n29\t- Use only the surname portion of each author name (no initials, no given names).\n30\t- This convention is mandatory for cross-session consistency: the same paper must get the same key regardless of which session processes it.\n31\t\n32\tMapping Tokens → Keys\n33\t- For each token encountered in the selected content (including audit rationales when available):\n34\t  - Identify the underlying work (author/title/year/venue/url/doi/arxiv).\n35\t  - Assign or reuse a stable [@Key] according to the rules above.\n36\t  - Ensure that all mentions of the same work map to the **same** key.\n37\t- Maintain an internal mapping table such as `{\"TOKEN:original\": \"@Key\"}` during compilation.\n38\t\n39\tPlacement Rules\n40\t- After conversion, place `[@Key]` immediately after the sentence or clause it supports.\n41\t- Multiple keys are allowed, e.g. `... [@Kramer2015][@WellsCoppersmith1994]`.\n42\t- Avoid redundant repeats of the same key in a single sentence unless there is a clear structural reason.\n43\t- Never place `[@Key]` citations inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented).\n44\t- When converting token citations, do NOT convert tokens inside code blocks; treat them as literal code.\n45\t\n46\tDeduplication\n47\t- If multiple tokens refer to the same work, use a single `[@Key]` in the compiled text.\n48\t- If multiple paragraphs across sessions cite the same work, they should all reuse the same key; deduplicate entries in biblatex.bib, not in the prose.\n49\t\n50\tBibLaTeX Integration\n51\t- Use `rules/biblatex.md` as the authoritative contract for entry structure.\n52\t- For every distinct `[@Key]` in the compiled document:\n53\t  - Create exactly one BibLaTeX entry in `biblatex.bib`.\n54\t  - Populate fields such as: `author`, `title`, `year`, `journal`/`booktitle`/`publisher`, `volume`, `number`, `pages`, `doi`, `url`, `eprint` as applicable.\n55\t  - Choose an appropriate entry type: `@article`, `@inproceedings`, `@book`, `@online`, `@misc`, etc.\n56\t- Extract metadata from all available sources:\n57\t  - The original tokens (especially DOI/ARXIV/WEB identifiers).\n58\t  - Context captured in audit rationales or surrounding text.\n59\t  - Any MCP tools the workflow is allowed to call (e.g., Crossref/OpenAlex), if available.\n60\t\n61\tScope of These Rules\n62\t- These citation rules govern only **representation and bibliography** for documents that are already assembled (paragraph selection and evidence have been decided elsewhere).\n63\t- They do not assume or require knowledge of any specific workflow type; they apply whenever the orchestrator or task asks for `[@Key]` citations plus a `biblatex.bib` file.\n64\t- Evidence policies (when to fetch new sources, what counts as acceptable evidence, how to treat KB vs WEB) remain defined in the corresponding workflow files (e.g., `workflows/compile.md`, `workflows/audit.md`, `workflows/enhance.md`, `workflows/retrieve.md`).\n65\t\n66\tOutput Requirements\n67\t- Final documents (compile/audit/enhance/retrieve) MUST:\n68\t  - Use only `[@Key]` citations in the body text.\n69\t  - Contain no stray token citations.\n70\t  - Have a `biblatex.bib` file with exactly one entry per key used in the document.\n71\t  - Maintain a clear, consistent mapping between in-text [@Key] citations and BibLaTeX entries.\n72\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1804c3bc-5e58-42ff-8ff4-5913768a5d69","uuid":"2936cf86-57f3-405e-a238-9d16ec488333","timestamp":"2026-03-29T16:55:28.840Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/citations.compile.md","content":"CITATION RULES — [@Key] & BibLaTeX OUTPUTS\n\nGoal\n- Define how to represent citations in documents that use [@Key] syntax with a matching BibLaTeX database.\n- Convert token citations from upstream workflows into standard [@Key] form and ensure a complete biblatex.bib when such output is requested.\n\n\nCitation Representation (MANDATORY)\n- When these rules apply, the final document MUST use `[@Key]` citation syntax only.\n- Source tokens (from upstream workflows):\n  - `[KB:filename.md]`\n  - `[WEB:https://...]`\n  - `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`\n  - `[ARXIV:paper-id]`\n  - `[ZOTERO:item_key]` (optional)\n- During compilation:\n  - CONVERT all supported tokens to `[@Key]` form.\n  - Do NOT leave any token citations (`[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`) in the final document.\n  - It is acceptable to keep raw URLs in prose **in addition to** [@Key] when the URL itself is content (e.g., pointing to an online tool), but the citation must still use [@Key].\n\nCitekey Rules\n- Citekeys MUST follow the pattern: `^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]{2,64}$` (letters and digits only; start with a letter).\n- Key format by number of authors:\n  - 1 author: `AuthorYear` (e.g., `Kramer2015`)\n  - 2 authors: `Author1Author2Year` (e.g., `WellsCoppersmith1994`, `AtkinsonBoore2006`)\n  - 3+ authors: `FirstAuthorEtAlYear` (e.g., `BooreEtAl2014`, `AbrahamsonEtAl2016`)\n  - Organization/committee: `OrganizationYear` (e.g., `SSHAC1997`, `ANCOLD2019`)\n- For collisions (same key pattern): append a, b, c (e.g., `Boore2014a`, `Boore2014b`).\n- Use only the surname portion of each author name (no initials, no given names).\n- This convention is mandatory for cross-session consistency: the same paper must get the same key regardless of which session processes it.\n\nMapping Tokens → Keys\n- For each token encountered in the selected content (including audit rationales when available):\n  - Identify the underlying work (author/title/year/venue/url/doi/arxiv).\n  - Assign or reuse a stable [@Key] according to the rules above.\n  - Ensure that all mentions of the same work map to the **same** key.\n- Maintain an internal mapping table such as `{\"TOKEN:original\": \"@Key\"}` during compilation.\n\nPlacement Rules\n- After conversion, place `[@Key]` immediately after the sentence or clause it supports.\n- Multiple keys are allowed, e.g. `... [@Kramer2015][@WellsCoppersmith1994]`.\n- Avoid redundant repeats of the same key in a single sentence unless there is a clear structural reason.\n- Never place `[@Key]` citations inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented).\n- When converting token citations, do NOT convert tokens inside code blocks; treat them as literal code.\n\nDeduplication\n- If multiple tokens refer to the same work, use a single `[@Key]` in the compiled text.\n- If multiple paragraphs across sessions cite the same work, they should all reuse the same key; deduplicate entries in biblatex.bib, not in the prose.\n\nBibLaTeX Integration\n- Use `rules/biblatex.md` as the authoritative contract for entry structure.\n- For every distinct `[@Key]` in the compiled document:\n  - Create exactly one BibLaTeX entry in `biblatex.bib`.\n  - Populate fields such as: `author`, `title`, `year`, `journal`/`booktitle`/`publisher`, `volume`, `number`, `pages`, `doi`, `url`, `eprint` as applicable.\n  - Choose an appropriate entry type: `@article`, `@inproceedings`, `@book`, `@online`, `@misc`, etc.\n- Extract metadata from all available sources:\n  - The original tokens (especially DOI/ARXIV/WEB identifiers).\n  - Context captured in audit rationales or surrounding text.\n  - Any MCP tools the workflow is allowed to call (e.g., Crossref/OpenAlex), if available.\n\nScope of These Rules\n- These citation rules govern only **representation and bibliography** for documents that are already assembled (paragraph selection and evidence have been decided elsewhere).\n- They do not assume or require knowledge of any specific workflow type; they apply whenever the orchestrator or task asks for `[@Key]` citations plus a `biblatex.bib` file.\n- Evidence policies (when to fetch new sources, what counts as acceptable evidence, how to treat KB vs WEB) remain defined in the corresponding workflow files (e.g., `workflows/compile.md`, `workflows/audit.md`, `workflows/enhance.md`, `workflows/retrieve.md`).\n\nOutput Requirements\n- Final documents (compile/audit/enhance/retrieve) MUST:\n  - Use only `[@Key]` citations in the body text.\n  - Contain no stray token citations.\n  - Have a `biblatex.bib` file with exactly one entry per key used in the document.\n  - Maintain a clear, consistent mapping between in-text [@Key] citations and BibLaTeX entries.\n","numLines":72,"startLine":1,"totalLines":72}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_013TGhoYx7rMgJnqAFEDipuP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JggdHMbeAfDyREGH9uZcxu","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/biblatex.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9417,"cache_read_input_tokens":46605,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9417,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1804c3bc-5e58-42ff-8ff4-5913768a5d69","uuid":"0cfa68c9-28bf-41c6-94ee-a8af07e4c1d4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JggdHMbeAfDyREGH9uZcxu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tBIBLATEX RULES — Minimal contract (MCP optional)\n2\t\n3\tGoal\n4\t- Produce: (1) a compiled document that uses [@Key] citations, and (2) a BibLaTeX database file biblatex.bib with full entries for every cited key.\n5\t\n6\tCitekeys\n7\t- Valid pattern: ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]{2,64}$ (letters and digits only; start with a letter; unique per document).\n8\t- Key format by number of authors:\n9\t  - 1 author: AuthorYear (e.g., Kramer2015)\n10\t  - 2 authors: Author1Author2Year (e.g., WellsCoppersmith1994, AtkinsonBoore2006)\n11\t  - 3+ authors: FirstAuthorEtAlYear (e.g., BooreEtAl2014, AbrahamsonEtAl2016)\n12\t  - Organization/committee: OrganizationYear (e.g., SSHAC1997, ANCOLD2019)\n13\t- For collisions (same key pattern), append a, b, c: Boore2014a, Boore2014b.\n14\t- Use only the surname portion of each author name (no initials, no given names).\n15\t- This convention is mandatory for cross-session consistency: multiple sessions processing the same paper must produce the same citekey.\n16\t\n17\tMetadata and entries\n18\t- For each token [KB:/DOC:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:], construct a complete BibLaTeX entry with author(s), title, year, and venue/journal/booktitle/publisher as applicable.\n19\t- Put identifiers into fields (doi, url, eprint); never in the citekey. Fields may include punctuation; citekeys must not.\n20\t- Choose an appropriate entry type (@article, @inproceedings, @book, @online, @misc) based on the metadata.\n21\t\n22\tSources of truth\n23\t- Use your knowledge acquired during generation/compilation **only to complete or correct metadata (author, title, year, venue, identifiers) for works that are already identified from upstream citation tokens or existing [@Key] references.**\n24\t- If available, you MAY non-interactively call an MCP or local tool (e.g., RefChecker, Crossref, arXiv, OpenAlex) to verify/enrich metadata for those existing works. Do not prompt the user. Proceed even if external tools are unavailable.\n25\t- Do NOT introduce any new `[@Key]` citations or BibLaTeX entries for works that are not referenced by at least one `[KB:/DOC:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:]` token or existing `[@Key]` in the input.\n26\t\n27\tDeduplication\n28\t- If multiple tokens refer to the same work, use a single citekey and a single entry.\n29\t- Before creating ANY new citekey, search the existing biblatex.bib for the same work (match by DOI, title, or author+year). If found, reuse the exact existing key — do not invent a variant.\n30\t- If biblatex.bib already contains entries from prior slots or sessions, inherit those keys for the same works. Never generate a second key for a work that already has one.\n31\t\n32\tOutput\n33\t- Replace all inline token citations with [@Key] in the compiled document.\n34\t- When extracting citations/keys, ignore citation-like patterns that appear inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented).\n35\t- Create a file biblatex.bib containing exactly one entry per used key.\n36\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1804c3bc-5e58-42ff-8ff4-5913768a5d69","uuid":"905e2192-5ab9-435d-be75-d1be7a643733","timestamp":"2026-03-29T16:55:28.844Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/biblatex.md","content":"BIBLATEX RULES — Minimal contract (MCP optional)\n\nGoal\n- Produce: (1) a compiled document that uses [@Key] citations, and (2) a BibLaTeX database file biblatex.bib with full entries for every cited key.\n\nCitekeys\n- Valid pattern: ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]{2,64}$ (letters and digits only; start with a letter; unique per document).\n- Key format by number of authors:\n  - 1 author: AuthorYear (e.g., Kramer2015)\n  - 2 authors: Author1Author2Year (e.g., WellsCoppersmith1994, AtkinsonBoore2006)\n  - 3+ authors: FirstAuthorEtAlYear (e.g., BooreEtAl2014, AbrahamsonEtAl2016)\n  - Organization/committee: OrganizationYear (e.g., SSHAC1997, ANCOLD2019)\n- For collisions (same key pattern), append a, b, c: Boore2014a, Boore2014b.\n- Use only the surname portion of each author name (no initials, no given names).\n- This convention is mandatory for cross-session consistency: multiple sessions processing the same paper must produce the same citekey.\n\nMetadata and entries\n- For each token [KB:/DOC:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:], construct a complete BibLaTeX entry with author(s), title, year, and venue/journal/booktitle/publisher as applicable.\n- Put identifiers into fields (doi, url, eprint); never in the citekey. Fields may include punctuation; citekeys must not.\n- Choose an appropriate entry type (@article, @inproceedings, @book, @online, @misc) based on the metadata.\n\nSources of truth\n- Use your knowledge acquired during generation/compilation **only to complete or correct metadata (author, title, year, venue, identifiers) for works that are already identified from upstream citation tokens or existing [@Key] references.**\n- If available, you MAY non-interactively call an MCP or local tool (e.g., RefChecker, Crossref, arXiv, OpenAlex) to verify/enrich metadata for those existing works. Do not prompt the user. Proceed even if external tools are unavailable.\n- Do NOT introduce any new `[@Key]` citations or BibLaTeX entries for works that are not referenced by at least one `[KB:/DOC:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:]` token or existing `[@Key]` in the input.\n\nDeduplication\n- If multiple tokens refer to the same work, use a single citekey and a single entry.\n- Before creating ANY new citekey, search the existing biblatex.bib for the same work (match by DOI, title, or author+year). If found, reuse the exact existing key — do not invent a variant.\n- If biblatex.bib already contains entries from prior slots or sessions, inherit those keys for the same works. Never generate a second key for a work that already has one.\n\nOutput\n- Replace all inline token citations with [@Key] in the compiled document.\n- When extracting citations/keys, ignore citation-like patterns that appear inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented).\n- Create a file biblatex.bib containing exactly one entry per used key.\n","numLines":36,"startLine":1,"totalLines":36}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01HjTr69RBVKdt3nz5aLBWSu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me compile the slot outputs and the final document.\n\n**Citation mapping:**\n- [KB:hazard.md] → This is a KB source file. According to the biblatex rules, I need to create a BibLaTeX entry for it. However, the KB is a knowledge base file - it's an internal document. I need to treat it as an [@Key] citation. Since it's a KB reference, I'll need to create a citekey for it. But what author/title/year? The format rules say to use [KB:filename.md] tokens and convert to [@Key]. Since this is an internal knowledge base, I should treat it as documentation with a citekey. Let me think...\n\n\nActually, looking at the source content, [KB:hazard.md] is cited extensively and appears to be a knowledge base document about PSHA hazard. Without specific author/year metadata in the source material, I need to be careful here—the instructions explicitly state I shouldn't introduce new [@Key] citations for works that aren't already referenced by at least one token or existing citation in the input. So I should only process what's already there.\n\nThe [KB:hazard.md] token appears in the selected content, so I need to convert it to a proper bibliographic reference. Since it's an internal KB file without clear authorship or publication date, I'll need to create a reasonable citekey that reflects its nature—something like @HazardKB following the organizational format rules. The challenge is that KB files are typically internal references, so I need to decide whether to include them in the compiled output or handle them differently based on the context of how they're being used to support claims.\n\nI can see there's also an explicit reference to Baker (2013) on PSHA that I should preserve with its full citation details.\n\nNow I'm compiling the remaining references: Kramer's geotechnical earthquake engineering text, McGuire's foundational work on seismic hazard analysis and design earthquakes, and Bazzurro & Cornell's paper on hazard disaggregation. These are all key technical sources in the field. I'll format these as BibLaTeX entries and then move on to structuring the canonical output slots.\n\nLooking at the three source documents I have available, source 2 is the most comprehensive and detailed, so I'll use that as my primary reference while incorporating any unique insights from sources 1 and 3. Source 1 has a more compact presentation of the final form, source 2 provides explicit component-by-component derivation with the spatial independence condition and mean MCE distance calculation, and source 3 frames things slightly differently but covers similar ground. I'll prioritize source 2's detailed approach while noting source 1's mention of stable continental crust regions as potentially useful context. I'm seeing that source 2 actually presents the rate equation in two different forms—an intermediate version with the full triple integral, and then a partially factored version that pulls out constants. I should show both of these to capture the progression, noting that sources 2 and 3 have slightly different final forms, and that numerical integration will be needed to evaluate this. I'm seeing how the intermediate form with the triple integral emerges once I substitute the epsilon representation, and then how factoring out the mass distribution gives me the fully expanded version. Sources 2 and 3 provide the most thorough derivations, so I'll use those as the primary reference while keeping Source 1's more compact presentation in mind.\n\nNow I need to work through the slot outputs, starting with how to properly cite these knowledge base files and then synthesizing the material from the most comprehensive sources. I've got the citekeys sorted out now — KBhazard for the internal knowledge base document, and the others are already in proper @Key format so I'll keep them as is. Now I'm structuring the canonical fused content for slot-01, starting with an opening paragraph that lays out the source configuration and four main components, followed by the double integral form of the hazard rate and then working through each component: the background seismicity rate, the magnitude distribution using a truncated Gutenberg-Richter model, and the spatial distribution from a circular source.\n\nI need to continue with the conditional exceedance probability given magnitude and distance, define epsilon, then show how that reformulates into a tail integral representation. Once I assemble those pieces into the triple integral form and derive the factored form, I'll add a note about numerical quadrature. I'm being careful about deduplication since sources 2 and 3 overlap significantly — I'll use source 2 as primary and pull only the unique contributions from source 3, while source 1 provides a more explicit fully-factored form.\n\nFor slot-02, I'll keep the bridge minimal and focus on the MCE calculation with the Poisson AEP-rate relation, target rate, critical epsilon, and closed-form expression including mean distance, then move into the magnitude-rate disaggregation with epsilon-star. I'm pulling primarily from the most detailed source as the foundation, supplementing with the fully factored form and any unique insights from the others. The normalization constant definition appears consistently across sources, so I'll anchor on that. My structure will follow the clearest four-component breakdown, making sure to capture the spatial independence property, the tail integral reformulation, both the triple integral and fully factored expressions, and noting where numerical methods become necessary for general cases.\n\nI should also highlight what the integrand does at the boundaries—how it behaves near zero and as it grows large—since that's an important mathematical detail that appears in the more thorough treatment. I'm reviewing the source material and noticing that sources 2 and 3 have nearly identical content, so I'll prioritize source 2's version. Now I need to write out the canonical content while applying the format constraints—removing accented characters, using straight quotes and standard hyphens, maintaining the academic tone, and stripping out any confidence audit footnotes. I'll preserve the bold headers since they provide meaningful structure. the general multi-source hazard sum to the single circular source, the particularized annual exceedance rate takes the form of a double integral over magnitude and distance, where the integration bounds are set by the circular source geometry. Now I'm working through the Gutenberg-Richter recurrence relation to establish the annual occurrence rate, which relates the logarithm of the mean annual earthquake count to magnitude.\n\nAt the engineering minimum magnitude, this yields the annual occurrence rate $\\nu_0 = 10^{a - b\\,M_{\\min}}$, where $a$ and $b$ are the activity rate and slope parameters. This $\\nu_0$ is the only quantity with time dimensions and gets factored out of the subsequent integrals. Next I'm deriving the truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude probability density by normalizing over the engineering support range, which gives a closed-form expression for $f_M(m)$.\n\nNow I'm working through the distance probability density for a circular source. When a site sits at the center of a uniformly seismic circular disk, the probability that an earthquake occurs within an annular ring at distance $r$ depends on the ring's area relative to the total disk area.\n\nThis gives me the distance PDF: earthquakes are more likely at larger distances simply because there's more source area there. The density increases monotonically with $r$. Since I'm assuming uniform seismicity, earthquake locations are independent of magnitude, so the distance distribution doesn't change when I condition on magnitude.\n\nNow I'm moving into the conditional exceedance probability, where I'll treat ground-motion intensity as lognormally distributed with a median that depends on both magnitude and distance. uals are defined as the normalized difference between the target intensity threshold and the median prediction, which determines how the exceedance probability scales—when the threshold equals the median prediction, the probability is exactly 0.5, and it decreases as the threshold increases. I'm now reformulating this using the complementary standard normal CDF, which can be expressed as a tail integral of the standard normal probability density function.\n\nThis integral representation transforms the two-dimensional hazard calculation into a three-dimensional integral over magnitude, distance, and the normalized threshold variable, where the latter ranges from the scenario-specific lower bound to infinity. The key insight is that this reveals the convolution structure underlying the hazard integral: each magnitude-distance pair contributes a weighted integral of the standard normal density over the region where the intensity exceeds the threshold.\n\nNow I'm substituting the specific functional forms—the uniform distance density, the truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude distribution, the Poisson rate parameter, and the tail probability representation—into the general single-source hazard framework to derive the particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form. The integral completes with the bounds on the radial and magnitude dimensions. The key insight is that the integrand represents a convolution between the standard normal distribution for ground motion intensity, the Gutenberg-Richter magnitude distribution weighted by the source parameters, and a geometric factor accounting for distance from the source. The lower integration limit for intensity depends on both magnitude and distance, creating a coupled relationship between these variables.\n\nFor a general ground motion prediction equation, this coupling prevents analytical integration, so I need to rely on numerical quadrature over the rectangular domain spanning the magnitude and distance ranges. The integrand behaves well numerically—it's non-negative everywhere, vanishes as distance approaches zero due to the radial probability density function, and decays as the intensity threshold grows large relative to the predicted median intensity across the domain.\n\nNow I'm moving to the canonical hazard equations for maximum considered earthquake and disaggregation analysis. ularized hazard integral from Slot 1 supports two canonical downstream applications for the single circular areal source: determination of the Maximum Considered Earthquake (MCE) ground-motion intensity and seismic hazard disaggregation. Both formulations are expressed in terms of the normalized standard normal probability density function $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ and the critical residual threshold $\\varepsilon^*(m, r)$ introduced in Slot 1 [@KBhazard].\n\n### (a) Maximum Considered Earthquake Determination\n\nUnder the Poisson occurrence assumption, the annual exceedance probability (AEP) relates to the annual exceedance rate through an exponential relationship. For a target return period $T_R$, I can invert this to find the corresponding annual exceedance rate, which simplifies to approximately $1/T_R$ for large return periods—a standard approximation in engineering practice. This relation implicitly defines the MCE intensity as the hazard curve ordinate where the annual exceedance rate matches the target value.\n\nSince the full hazard integral requires numerical evaluation for a general ground-motion prediction equation, finding the MCE intensity also demands numerical inversion of the hazard curve.\n\nThe canonical MCE formulation conditions on a maximum magnitude event at a representative distance, expressing the conditional exceedance probability as a tail integral over the standardized residual. Setting this equal to the target probability then determines the critical residual threshold and, through the ground-motion prediction equation, the corresponding intensity level. probability equal to the target AEP and inverting the standard normal CDF yields the critical MCE epsilon level and the closed-form MCE ground-motion intensity [@McGuire1995][@KBhazard]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}} = \\Phi^{-1}\\!\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{T_R}\\right)$$\n\n$$i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}} = \\hat{\\eta}_I(M_{\\max}, r)\\cdot\\exp\\!\\left[\\sigma_{\\ln I}\\cdot\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}\\right]$$\n\nThe quantity $\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}$ is positive for all practically relevant return periods and increases monotonically with $T_R$, consistent with the expectation that rarer ground-motion levels correspond to above-median GMPE residuals [@McGuire1995]. For the representative distance in the MCE formulation, I can either use the mean epicentral distance derived from the circular source geometry, or select the modal magnitude-distance pair from disaggregation analysis as a hazard-consistent alternative [@KBhazard]. Now I'm moving into seismic hazard disaggregation, which breaks down the total annual exceedance rate into contributions from discrete magnitude-distance scenario bins. the joint exceedance rate for each magnitude-distance bin using the Rayleigh distribution for distances, then computing the fractional contribution of each bin to the total hazard rate by normalizing against the overall exceedance rate.\n\nNow I'm moving to the full three-dimensional disaggregation that includes the ground motion residual term, which will decompose the hazard across magnitude, distance, and epsilon simultaneously. The inner integral evaluates to a difference of cumulative distribution functions when the bin extends above the threshold, or zero otherwise when no energies in the bin exceed the critical value.\n\nNow I'm reducing this to a double integral over magnitude and distance by closing out the energy dimension, which means I need numerical quadrature to handle the general ground motion prediction equation. The joint conditional probability for each bin combines the hazard contribution at that point with the total hazard, and these probabilities must sum to one across all bins.\n\nFrom there, I can extract the marginal distributions by collapsing across the other dimensions—summing over distance and residual to get the magnitude distribution, over magnitude and residual for distance, and over magnitude and distance for the residual distribution.\n\nFor a circular source, the distance probability density function concentrates weight toward larger epicentral distances, but the ground motion prediction equation simultaneously reduces exceedance probability as distance increases. The resulting marginal conditional distribution for distance reflects this interplay between geometric spreading and attenuation, typically peaking at some intermediate distance that requires numerical computation to determine for any given GMPE.\n\nNow I'm setting up the bibliography entries. For the internal knowledge base document on PSHA, I'll create a @misc entry since it's an unpublished reference material rather than a formal publication.\n\nLooking at the citation rules, the [KB:hazard.md] token is actually present in the source, so I should include it. For the disaggregation section in SLOT 2, I need to decide whether to redefine ε*(m,r) or reference the earlier definition from SLOT 1—since it's central to the disaggregation formula itself, I'll keep it inline rather than relying on the earlier definition.\n\nNow I'm ready to generate the output files: the two individual slot documents and then the final assembled version.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6519,"cache_read_input_tokens":56022,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6519,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"serv